        ("Does any of this apply to mixed doubles?",
         "Partly, but mixed doubles has its own conventions and this guide does not cover them. BWF's coaching material treats mixed as a separate case, with the pair favouring front-and-back for longer and different rules about who covers which part of the court. If you play mixed regularly, treat what follows as the level-doubles baseline rather than the finished answer."),
        ("What about left-handed pairs?",
         "Every source we could find assumes two right-handers, including the coaching manuals. Nobody appears to have published guidance on how a left-and-right pair should adjust their defensive sides or who should take the middle. The principles here still apply, but the specific which-side advice may not, and we would rather say so than invent an answer."),
        ("Am I allowed to shout \"mine\" to my partner?",
         "Yes. Law 13.4.5 makes it a fault to \"deliberately distract an opponent by any action such as shouting or making gestures\". The word doing the work there is deliberately, and it is about opponents. Calling to your own partner is normal play, and pairs who call have fewer shuttles land between them."),
        ("Do we have to swap sides after we win a point?",
         "Only for serving. The right and left service courts change with the score for whoever is serving, under Laws 11.1.1 and 11.1.2. Once the service has been returned, Law 11.2 lets either player hit the shuttle \"from any position on that player's side of the net\". Where you stand during a rally has nothing to do with the score."),
