<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px">

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead"><strong>There are two formations, not four.</strong> Front-and-back when your side is hitting the shuttle down, side-by-side when you have had to hit it up. That is the whole system. The difficulty is never remembering the two shapes, it is switching between them the moment the rally changes hands, and working out who goes where when you have never played with your partner before. If you are suddenly forced to defend and there is no time to think, the rule is: whoever was at the front picks a side, and whoever was at the back takes the other one.</p>

      <p>Get that wrong and you will spend the evening watching shuttles land in the gap between you, which is the most common way club pairs lose points they should have won.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:30px">
      <h4>The short version</h4>
      <ul style="margin:10px 0 0">
        <li><strong>Two formations.</strong> Front-and-back attacking, side-by-side defending. Coaching sources that inflate this to four or five are counting something else, or citing nothing.</li>
        <li><strong>Shuttle down, you attack. Shuttle up, you defend.</strong> At club level that rule has an exception worth knowing about.</li>
        <li><strong>Forced to defend?</strong> Front player picks a side, back player takes the other. It needs no agreement, so it works with a stranger.</li>
        <li><strong>Defending side-by-side is not a straight line.</strong> Slide across towards the shuttle together, and the cross-court player should stand further forward.</li>
        <li><strong>Nobody agrees who takes the middle.</strong> Settle it between you in ten seconds before you start.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:22px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem">Written for UK club-level doubles with two right-handers of roughly similar standard. Law references are to the Laws of Badminton as at August 2026.</p>

    <div class="guide-toc" style="margin-top:26px">
      <h4>In this guide</h4>
      <a href="#how-many">Two formations, not four</a>
      <a href="#laws">Where do the Laws say you must stand?</a>
      <a href="#which-shape">How do you tell which shape you should be in?</a>
      <a href="#which-side">Forced to defend: which side do you take?</a>
      <a href="#not-a-line">Defending is not a straight line</a>
      <a href="#middle">Who takes the shuttle down the middle?</a>
      <a href="#serve">Where do you stand for the serve and the return?</a>
      <a href="#partner">What if your partner does not move at all?</a>
      <a href="#does-it-matter">Does any of this matter on a social club night?</a>
      <a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:34px">

      <h2 id="how-many">Two formations, not four</h2>
      <p>Two. The BWF's own coach education says so plainly: the Level 2 player assessment criteria ask a player to <em>"describe 2 doubles formations"</em>, and the Level 1 manual names them, saying pairs <em>"use front and back as their attacking formation and side by side as their defensive formation"</em>. Every established coaching reference we checked agrees.</p>
      <p>You will still see articles promising four formations, or five. Two things are going on. Counting four usually means counting <strong>match-ups</strong> rather than formations: with two shapes and two sides of the net there are four combinations, and a table of those four is easy to misread as a list of four formations. Counting five generally means an article with no sources at all. One such piece, inventing formations called "diagonal", "hybrid" and "situational", is currently republished word for word across several unrelated websites, at least one of which otherwise sells drones. Those three names appear in no coaching material we could find.</p>
      <p>The frame that fits the sources is <strong>two formations and three situations</strong>. The formations are the two shapes. The situations are attacking, defending, and neutral: the flat midcourt exchange where nobody has the attack yet and both pairs are fighting to get it. Neutral is a situation you handle by choosing one of the two shapes, not a third shape of its own.</p>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
        <img src="/img/diagrams/doubles-attack-defence.svg" alt="A badminton court from above. The attacking pair stand in a line down the middle of their half, one at the net and one at the back. The defending pair stand side by side, one covering each side of their half." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">The two shapes. Which one you are in is decided by who is hitting down, not by preference.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="laws">Where do the Laws say you must stand?</h2>
      <p>Almost nowhere, and this surprises people. The Laws constrain the positions of exactly two players, the server and the receiver, and only until the serve is struck. They must <em>"stand within diagonally opposite service courts without touching the boundary lines"</em> (Law 9.1.3) and keep both feet stationary and in contact with the floor until the service is delivered (Law 9.1.4).</p>
      <p>Their partners are free. Law 9.5 is explicit: <em>"in doubles, during the delivery of service, the partners may take up any positions within their respective courts, which do not unsight the opposing server or receiver."</em> So where your partner stands at the serve is entirely a tactical choice, not a rule you are obeying.</p>
      <p>Once the rally is underway, even the server and receiver are released. Law 11.2 allows the shuttle to be hit <em>"from any position on that player's side of the net"</em>. Two club misconceptions die here: the score does not dictate where you stand during a rally, and there is no requirement to take alternate shots with your partner.</p>

      <h2 id="which-shape">How do you tell which shape you should be in?</h2>
      <p>Look at which way the shuttle is travelling. If your side is hitting it downwards you are attacking, so get into front-and-back. If you have had to hit it upwards you are defending, so get side-by-side. A club coaching document from Cambridge puts the test simply: <em>"you can tell which team is attacking because they will be hitting the shuttle down, whereas the defending team will be hitting it up."</em></p>
      <p>The exception matters more at club level than anywhere else, and the same document names it: <em>"a weak player may hit the shuttle down so slowly and flatly that the opposition are able to smash it."</em> A slow, floaty drop shot travels downwards and still hands over the attack. So the real test is not the direction of your shot. It is whether your opponents can hit down next. If they can, you are defending, whatever you just did.</p>

      <h2 id="which-side">Forced to defend: which side do you take?</h2>
      <p>This is the moment club pairs lose most, and it has a clean answer. You were attacking in a line, one at the net and one at the back. You have had to lift. Now you both need to be side-by-side, immediately, and you have no time to discuss it.</p>
      <p><strong>The player who was at the front picks a side. Either side. The player who was at the back takes the other one.</strong> It works because it is asymmetric: the player at the back can see their partner in front of them and can see which way they went, while the front player cannot see behind. So the person with the information is the one who adapts.</p>
      <p>That protocol comes from a real club's own teaching document, and its virtue is that it requires no agreement in advance. You can use it with someone you met four minutes ago, which at a club night is usually the situation. If there is no time even to choose deliberately, the same source has the fallback: <em>"just pick any side, and your partner, who can see which choice you made, will go to the other side."</em></p>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
        <img src="/img/diagrams/doubles-who-takes-which-side.svg" alt="A badminton court from above. A pair who were attacking in a front-and-back line have had to lift. The front player moves to one side and the back player, who can see that choice, moves to the other, finishing side by side." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">Whoever can see the other player's decision is the one who adapts. That is why this works without a conversation.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="not-a-line">Defending is not a straight line</h2>
      <p>Almost every diagram of side-by-side defence shows two players standing level with each other, each guarding half the width. That picture is wrong in two ways, and correcting it is the single biggest improvement most club pairs can make to their defence.</p>
      <p><strong>You move across together, not apart.</strong> The BWF Level 1 manual: <em>"for wider lifts, the pair move across together towards the same side as the shuttle has been lifted. This allows them to deal more effectively with straight or down-the-middle replies from the attacker, which are the most frequent returns."</em> You are not each defending a half. You are both shifting towards the danger, because that is where the shuttle is most likely to go.</p>
      <p><strong>And you do not stay level.</strong> The same manual: <em>"the cross-court player can take up a more advanced position, because cross-court strokes will take longer to reach her, so she will have more time to react."</em> The player furthest from the shuttle has more time, so they can afford to stand further up the court and cover the drop shot into the middle. The player on the shuttle's side stays wider and deeper, nearer their tramline, because a straight smash arrives fast.</p>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
        <img src="/img/diagrams/doubles-defence-asymmetric.svg" alt="A badminton court from above. The shuttle is being smashed straight down one side. Both defenders have slid across towards it rather than staying level. The defender on the shuttle's side is wide and deep near the tramline; the cross-court defender is nearer the centre line and a step further forward. Faded circles show the level positions most club pairs take instead." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">The faded circles are where most club pairs stand. Sliding across together, with the cross-court player further forward, covers the two replies the attacker is most likely to choose.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="middle">Who takes the shuttle down the middle?</h2>
      <p>There is no agreed rule, and you should know that rather than hunt for one. Three positions circulate and they contradict each other.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>"Forehand takes it."</strong> The most commonly repeated convention, and the easiest to remember.</li>
        <li><strong>"You defend on the backhand anyway."</strong> The argument against the first one, and it has physiology behind it: defending a smash aimed at your body with a forehand action is close to impossible, so doubles defence is played predominantly backhand, including down the middle. The clearest published statements of this are anonymous, so treat it as a strong convention rather than settled doctrine.</li>
        <li><strong>"Agree it beforehand."</strong> The advice from coaches who decline to pick between the first two. As one puts it, decide before the rally who owns the middle when you are defending.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Our view: take the third option, because it is the only one that survives being paired with a stranger. Ten seconds before you start, say "I'll take the middle" and let your partner disagree if they would rather. Any agreement beats the situation where both of you assume the other has it, which is how the shuttle ends up landing between you untouched.</p>

      <h2 id="serve">Where do you stand for the serve and the return?</h2>
      <p>Serving side: the server serves low and short from close to the front service line, and stays forward after serving, ready for a reply into the net. Their partner covers the back. You are already in the attacking shape before the rally starts, which is the point of a low serve.</p>
      <p>Receiving side: the receiver stands ready to take the serve early and high, and their partner covers the back. The single most common error in the whole of club doubles happens here, and it belongs to the partner, not the receiver. The Cambridge document identifies it: after a high serve, the problem is <em>"for the receiver's partner to forget to move to the front of the court"</em>, which leaves a large area open on the very next shot.</p>
      <p>If you take one habit from this section, make it that one. When your partner receives, watch what the serve does and move accordingly, rather than watching your partner play the shot.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:24px">
        <h4>Worked example: one rally, four changes</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">You serve low. You stay forward, your partner covers the back: <strong>front-and-back</strong>. They push it flat to the midcourt, nobody can hit down: <strong>neutral</strong>, and you both end up roughly level in the middle of the court. Your partner lifts it to the back: you are <strong>defending</strong>, so you pick a side and your partner takes the other, and you both slide towards whichever side they lift to. They smash straight, you block it to the net. Now they have to lift: you are <strong>attacking</strong> again, so whoever is nearer the net stays there and the other drops back.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:10px">Four changes in one rally, and not one of them needed a conversation. That is what the two formations are for.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="partner">What if your partner does not move at all?</h2>
      <p>Then you use the front-player-picks rule anyway, because it is one-sided by design. You do not need your partner's cooperation to make the right move yourself, and if they stay where they are, you have still covered a different part of the court from them, which is most of the benefit.</p>
      <p>Two things worth knowing when the pairing is not working smoothly. <strong>Do not turn round to watch your partner hit.</strong> If you are at the front, keep facing the net. Turning to look means taking a shuttle in the face at close range, and it is one of the few genuine injury risks in club badminton. <strong>And talk.</strong> Calling "mine" or "yours" is entirely legal: Law 13.4.5 faults a player who <em>"deliberately distracts an opponent"</em>, which is about opponents and about intent. Calling to your own partner is ordinary play.</p>

      <h2 id="does-it-matter">Does any of this matter on a social club night?</h2>
      <p>Less than the internet implies. Badminton England runs around a thousand No Strings sessions a week, explicitly for players of all abilities and built around fun rather than competition, and that is the format most people reading this are actually in. If you play once a week for enjoyment, the two shapes and the front-player-picks rule are the whole of what you need. The rest of this guide is detail for people who want to compete.</p>
      <p>It is worth noting that the club whose protocol we quoted above deliberately does not teach the professional method. Their document says so, with some cheerfulness: <em>"professional players have a different system, involving anticlockwise rotation, but we aren't that good."</em> A functioning club decided that a simpler rule its members could actually use beat a better rule they could not. That is a sound judgement, not a compromise, and it applies to most club nights.</p>
      <p>What we would not do is drill any of this. Positioning is the kind of thing that improves by being understood once and then noticed occasionally, not by being practised. If your club night is a social one, understanding it is the whole return.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h4>If you only do one thing</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">When you are forced to defend, the player who was at the front picks a side and the player who was at the back takes the other. You never have to agree it, discuss it, or know your partner.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:24px">
        <h4>Fair games, without the admin</h4>
        <p>Knowing where to stand helps most when the games are even in the first place. ePegboard picks balanced pairings on a club night and keeps the mixing varied, so people play with a range of partners rather than the same three. Free for clubs.</p>
